## Use unerr's tools, not bash or built-ins

unerr serves the live code graph and your team's rules via MCP tools — one query replaces 5-15 file reads for any code read, search, or edit.

| To… | Use |
|---|---|
| Find code, or pull context for a change | `search_code({query})` — a task phrase returns a recon bundle: focus entity (+ body if single-entity), callers (blast radius), top matches, conventions. More bodies: `file_read({entity})`, `include_body:true`, `cache_ref` (zero recompute). A bare symbol returns ranked matches. |
| Exact string / regex across files | `search_code({query, mode:'literal'\|'regex'})` — match + surrounding lines, no follow-up read |
| Read a file, range, entity (+ top callers), or structure | `file_read({file_path})` whole file; `{offset,limit}` a range; `{entity}` one entity + top callers; `{outline:true}` structure |
| Change a file | `file_edit` (`{old_string,new_string}` or `{content}`) — no prior read needed |
| Fetch a URL or docs | `fetch_url` (bulk: `{urls:[...]}`) |
| Caller/callee graph, or a rename sweep | `get_references({key, direction:'callers'\|'callees'})` — unlocks on first edit, or automatically at fan_in ≥ 5; add `include_text_occurrences:true` for a rename |

Pick the tool that fits — no required sequence.

## Keep `@sem` doc comments true in the same edit

Domain comment (Layer 8): when `file_edit` changes what an `@sem`-commented entity does or why, rewrite its prose summary and `@sem domain=<tag>` line in the SAME Edit call; NEVER delete an `@sem` comment unless the user instructs it.

## The hooks already do the protective work — don't pay a call to repeat it

Conventions and drift (on read) and the blast-radius gate (on edit) arrive on their own as `ur|<tag>` lines. Read them. Don't re-spend `search_code` / `get_references` / `file_read` on context already delivered.

## Delegate by default

Spawn `unerr-worker` (scoped coding from a spec) / `unerr-junior` (read-only investigation, verify-runs) sub-agents for delegable slices, in parallel, one per slice — the main thread plans, routes, and consolidates. The full tiering and task-tracker rules live in your instruction file, not here.

## Output discipline

Lead with the answer; structured summaries over prose; show diffs, not whole files; never repeat context already in the conversation.
